@PACKAGE_INIT@

# fixpp CMake package configuration.
#
# Usage:
#   find_package(fixpp REQUIRED)
#   target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE fixpp::fixpp)
#
# That is the whole contract. Include paths, compile features, link ordering and
# transitive third-party dependencies all arrive through the imported target; a
# consumer never hand-adds an include directory and never restates a link line.
#
# PROVIDER-AGNOSTIC (084 FR-018e). fixpp is BUILT with Conan, but nothing about
# that survives into this file: every fixpp target links only imported target
# names -- there is not a single find_library(... NO_DEFAULT_PATH) or absolute
# package-cache path anywhere in the exported graph -- so each find_dependency()
# below is a plain find_package() resolved against YOUR CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH, by
# whatever provider you use (distro packages, vcpkg, Conan, a manual prefix).
#
# find_dependency LOCATES, it does not PROVIDE. You must already have these
# available. Two of them are rarely offered by a platform package manager and you
# will most likely have to supply them yourself:
#   * Crc32c            -- rarely distro-packaged
#   * opentelemetry-cpp -- rarely distro-packaged, and required by EVERY artifact
#                          this project ships (all shipped configurations are
#                          built with the OpenTelemetry integration enabled)
#
# The list below is DERIVED AT CONFIGURE TIME from the built target graph, not
# hand-maintained: every export-set member's link interface is walked, including
# $<LINK_ONLY:> entries (a static library does not link its own private
# dependencies, so the consumer's final link must still resolve them). A missing
# entry therefore surfaces as a hard error inside find_package(fixpp) at CONFIGURE
# time, not as an undefined symbol at link time.

include(CMakeFindDependencyMacro)

@FIXPP_FIND_DEPENDENCY_BLOCK@
include("${CMAKE_CURRENT_LIST_DIR}/fixppTargets.cmake")

check_required_components(fixpp)
